@page "/help"
@attribute [Authorize]
@inject EnvironmentRegistry Environments

<PageTitle>Help &amp; guide · SecretManager</PageTitle>

<div class="page-header">
    <div>
        <h1>Help &amp; guide</h1>
        <p class="page-subtitle">How to set up passwords and connection details so an application can use them, in plain terms.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="row g-4">
    <div class="col-xl-7">
        <div class="card mb-4">
            <div class="card-header"><h3><i class="i i-zap"></i> Setting up a secret for someone</h3></div>
            <div class="card-body getting-started">
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <div><strong>Pick the environment.</strong> Open <a href="secrets">Secrets</a> and choose the tab for the environment the application runs in: Dev, Test, Upgrade or Prod. Each environment keeps its own separate copy.</div>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <div><strong>Add the secret.</strong> Press <em>Add</em>, choose what kind of secret it is (for example <em>SQL Server database</em> or <em>Website or API login</em>), and fill in the details you were given. Fields marked <span class="req">*</span> are required; the bar at the bottom tells you what's still missing.</div>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <div><strong>Test it</strong> (for databases). Press <em>Test</em> to check the server accepts the login before you save.</div>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <div><strong>Give the application access.</strong> After saving, press <em>Give an application access</em>. Choose an existing application or add a new one, and tick the secrets it may read.</div>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <div><strong>Hand over the key.</strong> You'll see the application's key once. Send it to the application's owner privately (for example through a password manager share). Copy the <em>setup instructions</em> as well; those are safe to email.</div>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card mb-4">
            <div class="card-header"><h3><i class="i i-refresh"></i> Common tasks</h3></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <dl class="glossary mb-0">
                    <dt>A password was changed on the database</dt>
                    <dd>Open the secret, turn on <em>Change login details</em>, enter the new password and save. Applications receive the new one within a few minutes. The old version is kept in <em>History</em> in case you need to go back.</dd>
                    <dt>The same database exists in Test and Prod</dt>
                    <dd>Create it in one environment, then use <em>Copy to…</em> on the secret. Settings are copied but the password is not, so enter the Prod password yourself.</dd>
                    <dt>An application stopped working</dt>
                    <dd>Check that its key hasn't expired or been turned off (Applications page), that it uses the right environment address, and that it was given access to the secret. Your administrator can look in the Activity log for the exact reason.</dd>
                    <dt>A key was lost or shared by mistake</dt>
                    <dd>On the Applications page press <em>Issue a new key</em> (the circular-arrows button). The old key stops working at once. Send the new key to the owner.</dd>
                    <dt>I need to see a password</dt>
                    <dd>Open the secret and press <em>Show</em>. It hides again after 30 seconds, and the view is recorded.</dd>
                </dl>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-xl-5">
        <div class="card mb-4">
            <div class="card-header"><h3><i class="i i-info"></i> Words used here</h3></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <dl class="glossary mb-0">
                    <dt>Secret</dt>
                    <dd>A password, database login or other private value, stored encrypted.</dd>
                    <dt>Environment</dt>
                    <dd>A separate copy of everything for each stage: Dev (development), Test, Upgrade (rehearsals before going live) and Prod (live). A secret in Dev can never be read by a Prod application, and vice versa.</dd>
                    <dt>Protected environment</dt>
                    <dd>@string.Join(" and ", Environments.All.Where(e => e.Protected).Select(e => e.Name)): you'll be asked for a reason, such as a change ticket number, before viewing or changing anything.</dd>
                    <dt>Folder</dt>
                    <dd>The first part of a secret's name, such as <code>billing</code> in <code>billing/main-database</code>. It groups related secrets, and lets you give an application access to the whole group at once.</dd>
                    <dt>Application</dt>
                    <dd>A program, service or scheduled job that reads secrets. It identifies itself with its <strong>key</strong>.</dd>
                    <dt>Key</dt>
                    <dd>A long code that works like a password for an application. It works in one environment only, and only for the secrets you allowed.</dd>
                    <dt>Connection string</dt>
                    <dd>The single line of text an application uses to connect to a database. SecretManager builds it for you from the details you enter.</dd>
                </dl>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card help-card">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h3 class="h6"><i class="i i-shield"></i> Good habits</h3>
                <ul class="small mb-0">
                    <li>Never send keys or passwords by email or chat. Use your password manager's share feature.</li>
                    <li>Give each application access to only what it needs: pick a folder or individual secrets rather than <em>everything</em>.</li>
                    <li>Add a description with an owner or team, so others know who to ask.</li>
                    <li>Set a password change reminder for important accounts.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
